
  Next, delivery — and there's a hard constraint here worth naming before you choose. With no server, nothing can notify
  you while the page is closed. Web Push requires a server to send from; the Notification Triggers API that would have
  allowed offline scheduling never shipped in any stable browser. So any "ping me when I'm not looking" behavior is
  genuinely blocked until the multi-user/server phase, not merely deferred.

  What that leaves:

  - [1mIn-app notification center[0m — a bell/inbox inside the page: unread count, list, mark-as-read. Works whenever the tab
    is open, which for a browser-only app is whenever you'd see it anyway. This is the durable core; every other surface
    is a transport on top of it.
  - [1mOS notifications while the tab is open[0m — the [38;5;153mNotification[39m API, gated behind a permission prompt. Real toast in the
    corner of your screen, but only while the page is loaded. Cheap to add once the center exists.
  - [1mIn-page toasts[0m — transient banners for things that fire while you're actively looking. Purely cosmetic layer over
    the same events.

  My recommendation: [1mbuild the center, plus OS notifications[0m. The center is the piece that has to exist for anything
  else to have data to show, and it's what carries forward unchanged when a server arrives. OS notifications are a thin
  adapter over it and give you the actual "ping" feeling. I'd skip toasts initially — they overlap heavily with the
  center for a single user and add a timing/stacking problem for little gain.

